1	# UTCP-MCP Bridge (Python)
2	
3	Python implementation of the UTCP-MCP Bridge for users who prefer Python or need specific Python environment features.
4	
5	## 🚀 Quick Start
6	
7	### 1. Install Dependencies
8	
9	Ensure you have [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/) installed on your system:
10	
11	```bash
12	# Using pipx (recommended)
13	pipx install uv
14	
15	# Using pip
16	pip install uv
17	
18	# Or using curl
19	curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh
20	```
21	
22	### 2. Download the Script
23	
24	Download the [`utcp-client-mcp.py`](utcp-client-mcp.py) file to your desired location.
25	
26	### 3. Configure Your MCP Client
27	
28	Add this configuration to your MCP client (Claude Desktop, etc.):
29	
30	```json
31	{
32	  "mcpServers": {
33	    "utcp-python": {
34	      "command": "uv",
35	      "args": [
36	        "run",
37	        "/path/to/utcp-client-mcp.py"
38	      ],
39	      "env": {
40	        "UTCP_CONFIG_FILE": "/path/to/your/.utcp_config.json"
41	      }
42	    }
43	  }
44	}
45	```
46	
47	## 🔧 Configuration
48	
49	Create a `.utcp_config.json` file in the same directory as the Python script:
50	
51	```json
52	{
53	    "load_variables_from": [
54	      {
55	        "variable_loader_type": "dotenv",
56	        "env_file_path": ".env"
57	      }
58	    ],
59	    "manual_call_templates": [
60	      {
61	          "name": "openlibrary",
62	          "call_template_type": "http",
63	          "http_method": "GET",
64	          "url": "https://openlibrary.org/static/openapi.json",
65	          "content_type": "application/json"
66	      }
67	    ],
68	    "post_processing": [
69	      {
70	          "tool_post_processor_type": "filter_dict",
71	          "only_include_keys": ["name", "description"],
72	          "only_include_tools": ["openlibrary.*"]
73	      }
74	    ],
75	    "tool_repository": {
76	      "tool_repository_type": "in_memory"
77	    },
78	    "tool_search_strategy": {
79	      "tool_search_strategy_type": "tag_and_description_word_match"
80	    }
81	}
82	```
83	
84	## 🛠️ Available MCP Tools
85	
86	The Python bridge exposes the same MCP tools as the Node.js version:
87	
88	- **`register_manual`** - Register new UTCP manuals/APIs
89	- **`deregister_manual`** - Remove registered manuals
90	- **`call_tool`** - Execute any registered UTCP tool
91	- **`search_tools`** - Find tools by description
92	- **`list_tools`** - List all registered tool names
93	- **`get_required_keys_for_tool`** - Get required environment variables
94	- **`tool_info`** - Get complete tool information and schema
95	
96	## 📋 Dependencies
97	
98	The Python script uses these dependencies (automatically handled by `uv`):
99	
100	- `fastmcp` - Fast MCP server implementation
101	- `utcp` - Core UTCP client
102	- `utcp-mcp` - MCP integration
103	- `utcp-text` - Text processing tools
104	- `utcp-cli` - Command-line tools
105	- `utcp-http` - HTTP tools
106	
107	## 🌟 Features
108	
109	- ✅ **Zero global installation** - Uses `uv run` for dependency isolation
110	- ✅ **Python ecosystem** - Perfect for Python-heavy workflows
111	- ✅ **Same functionality** - All features from the Node.js version
112	- ✅ **Environment isolation** - Each project can have its own config
113	- ✅ **Fast startup** - Optimized for quick tool loading
114	
115	## 🔄 Alternative: Using pip/pipx
116	
117	If you prefer not to use `uv`, you can also install dependencies globally:
118	
119	```bash
120	pip install fastmcp utcp utcp-mcp utcp-text utcp-cli utcp-http
121	```
122	
123	Then use this MCP configuration:
124	
125	```json
126	{
127	  "mcpServers": {
128	    "utcp-python": {
129	      "command": "python",
130	      "args": ["/path/to/utcp-client-mcp.py"]
131	    }
132	  }
133	}
134	```
135	
136	## 🆚 Why Choose Python Version?
137	
138	- **Python expertise** - If your team is more comfortable with Python
139	- **Custom Python tools** - Easy to extend with Python-specific functionality
140	- **Environment control** - More granular control over Python dependencies
141	- **Debugging** - Easier to debug and modify if you know Python
142	
143	For most users, we recommend the **Node.js version** via `npx @utcp/mcp-bridge` as it requires zero installation and works out of the box.
144	

